{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And she saw him, that he was good.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): it means that he was beautiful and pleasant. And even if he had been ugly, it is a mother's way to have compassion on her son and to protect him from danger and to hide him away, and Scripture would not have needed to say on this account 'that he was good.' Therefore the Sages, of blessed memory, needed to make a Midrash on this: that the excellence of goodness she saw in him was that he was born circumcised. And they further expounded of him that the entire house was filled with light, in the manner of what is said (Genesis 1:4): ״וירא אלהים את האור כי טוב״. And so the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded concerning the first man, who was the fashioned work of the hands of the Holy One, blessed be He, that He created him circumcised." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'that he was good' would have sufficed, according to this Midrash, for it to say 'and she saw him, that it was good' — since they derived it from the language of ״וירא אלהים את האור כי טוב״. But it added the word 'הוא' — for הוא is the Name of the Holy One, blessed be He, as is said (Psalms 100:3): ״הוא עשנו ולא אנחנו״; (Isaiah 42:8): ״אני ה' הוא שמי״. And that which the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded — that with the letter ה the world was created — the intent is the ה and the ו of the Name, because the Shekhinah is comprised of six extremities, as hinted in the psalm (Psalms 121:1): ״אשא עיני אל ההרים״, where David, peace be upon him, mentioned שמירה six times, which is a hint to the Shekhinah. And since the root of the letters and their existence is the letter אלף, we do not find the Name except with אלף. Therefore it stated here regarding Moses, 'that he was good' — הוא: with the word 'כי טוב' it hinted that the house was filled with light, and also in that it added the word 'הוא' it hinted to the Shekhinah that was seen with him. And this is what the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: ״ותפתח ותראהו את הילד״ — 'ותרא' should have been sufficient; rather, it teaches that she saw the Shekhinah with him. Here you have it explicitly that the masters of truth drew out for us a hint to the Shekhinah from the addition of the ה and ו of ותראהו. And so I say regarding the addition of 'כי טוב הוא': since logic dictates that if Bithiah daughter of Pharaoh merited to see the face of the Shekhinah for the glory of Moses, how much more so did his righteous mother merit this." "<b>And she hid him three months.</b> On the seventh of Adar Moses was born, and the Egyptians waited for the end of nine months, according to the way of the world, and they end on the sixth of Sivan. And therefore she could no longer hide him. And it says 'ירחים' and not 'חדשים,' because they are lunar months, which are two deficient and one full. And when you count twenty-nine, twenty-nine — two deficient months — that is fifty-eight (נ״ח); and one full month of thirty — that is eighty-eight days. And this falls on the sixth of Sivan, which is the day on which the Torah was given; on that very day he was cast into the river. And Scripture tells you that on the day he suffered in the river, on that day he was exalted in heaven and received the Torah from Sinai. And this is what David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 138:3): ״ביום קראתי ותענני תרהיבני בנפשי עז״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:29.502673385Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}